          "type": "turn",
          "question": "My son has been wheezing at night.",
          "answer": "**Urgency:** The symptom check returned a **routine** tier, and no emergency pattern matched your description. That is not a clearance. The check only covers a fixed list of presentations. Because this is your child, please arrange a medical review soon, not just at a later routine visit, especially if the wheezing is frequent or disrupting sleep.\n\n**Get emergency help right away if your son:**\n- Is struggling to breathe, or the skin pulls in between or under the ribs with each breath\n- Has blue or grey lips or face\n- Cannot speak in full sentences, or cries without enough breath\n- Cannot feed or drink\n- Becomes drowsy, hard to wake, or unusually limp or confused\n\nThose are the signs you listed, and I could not find a retrieved source that confirms this list for children. Treat them as reasons to call emergency services without waiting.\n\n**What the guidance I retrieved says:** The asthma passages describe wheeze, breathlessness, chest tightness and cough that often get worse at night or in the early morning, and that are set off by triggers such as exercise, allergens, cold air or viral infection (sources: \"Asthma\" and \"Asthma: confirming the diagnosis and the stepwise approach\"). Those passages are written for adults, and they do not cover how to assess wheeze in children. The other conditions the search suggested (migraine, insomnia, heart failure, reflux) do not fit wheezing well, so I have not weighed them. Only a clinician can work out what is causing your son's symptoms. Useful things to tell them include how often the wheezing happens, whether it comes with cough or colds, any known triggers, and whether he has eczema, hay fever or a family history of asthma.\n\nI could not complete a search for child-specific red flags, so please check the advice from your child's doctor or local health service, or call your local emergency number if you are worried.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
