          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Does his age change what is recommended?",
          "answer": "Yes, age often changes what is recommended. The passages I found show a few ways this happens:\n\n- **Vaccines:** Influenza guidance names children under 5, and especially under 2, among the groups that benefit most from vaccination. It also describes higher-dose vaccines for adults aged 65 and over. (Source: \"Influenza: annual vaccination and the timing of antiviral treatment\")\n- **Screening:** Diabetes guidance starts routine screening for adults at age 35. Younger adults are screened only if they have additional risk factors. (Source: \"Type 2 diabetes: who is screened, and the diagnostic thresholds\")\n- **Test ranges:** Some thyroid guidance says a mildly raised test result may be normal in older adults, so the reference range may need adjusting for age. (Source: \"Hypothyroidism: which test comes first, and the monitoring interval after a change\")\n- **Risk over a lifetime:** Blood pressure tends to rise with age because arteries stiffen over time. (Source: \"Hypertension (high blood pressure)\")\n\nNone of the passages I found covers how recommendations differ for a four-year-old with wheezing. The asthma passages describe symptoms in general terms and do not split guidance by age. So I cannot say whether his age changes the asthma advice.\n\nTo give a more specific answer, please tell me:\n1. The exact condition, test, vaccine or treatment you are asking about.\n2. The age of the person it concerns. You mentioned your son is four. Please confirm that the question is about him.\n\nA doctor or nurse who has seen him can tell you how age applies to his situation. If his breathing worsens or he shows any of the emergency signs I listed earlier, call your local emergency number now.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
